Exit Status / Return Code

Every Linux command returns a number called an exit status.

0 = success
Non-zero = failure/error

Eg:
#!/bin/bash

ls /home

echo "Exit status: $?"

a)If ls succeeds

Exit status: 0

b)If the command fails:
ls /abc
echo $?

You may get:

ls: cannot access '/abc': No such file or directory
2

c)Using exit status with if

#!/bin/bash

ls /home

if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "Command successful"
else
    echo "Command failed"
fi

=> Better approach

Instead of checking $? separately:

if ls /home; then
    echo "Command successful"
else
    echo "Command failed"
fi

=> Why is this useful for Linux Admin?

Suppose you restart a service:

systemctl restart ssh

You can check whether it succeeded:

if systemctl restart ssh; then
    echo "SSH service restarted successfully"
else
    echo "Failed to restart SSH"
fi
